WEBVTT

NOTE Captions for the Tuuyi introduction video, transcribed from the narration.

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.500
Software diligence often begins with a surprisingly simple problem.

2
00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:09.260
The seller says the product does something. Does the evidence support that claim?

3
00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:14.780
Tuuyi identifies the claims being made, checks them against the supplied code and documents,

4
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.200
independently checks the findings, and reports what the evidence supports.

5
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:25.520
The result is not just a conclusion. Each finding shows the claim, the evidence behind it,

6
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:30.320
and where that evidence came from, so the buyer can check it directly.

7
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.580
And after the report is delivered, the buyer can continue asking questions about the completed review.

8
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.700
Why focus on claims? Isn't technical diligence really about whether the code is good?

9
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.320
Sometimes. But early in a deal, the buyer is often relying on statements such as

10
00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:53.340
built for privacy, production ready, or our proprietary AI.

11
00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.760
Those statements affect what the buyer thinks they are buying.

12
00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.460
They can affect the price, the terms, the timetable, or even whether the deal makes sense.

13
00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:04.900
Tuuyi addresses that narrower question.

14
00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:10.260
Which claims the buyer is being asked to rely on are supported by the available evidence,

15
00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:16.360
which are not, and which still depend on trust, because the supplied material cannot settle them.

16
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:22.470
What if the seller never spells out every feature as a separate claim?

17
00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:29.470
They usually don't. Some claims are explicit, no telemetry, runs in Docker, supports PostgreSQL.

18
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.770
Others come from the ordinary meaning of how the seller describes the product.

19
00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.810
If you call something a URL shortener, for example,

20
00:01:39.490 --> 00:01:44.410
a reasonable buyer expects it to create short codes and resolve those codes to stored URLs.

21
00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:47.550
But that does not give us permission to invent requirements.

22
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:53.190
Calling it a URL shortener does not automatically promise analytics, custom domains, or an API.

23
00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:57.710
And vague descriptions such as simple, modern, or enterprise grade

24
00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:03.410
are not converted into testable claims merely because the words appear in the sales material.

25
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:08.900
Before examining the software, we establish the context of the review.

26
00:02:09.540 --> 00:02:14.880
Who is the buyer? What is being purchased? Which seller documents carry the claims that matter?

27
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.040
What does the buyer already know or suspect?

28
00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.500
And what kinds of findings would actually affect price, terms, or the decision to close?

29
00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.740
Those answers come from the buyer.

30
00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:30.040
The review does not fill in missing deal assumptions on its own.

31
00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:35.000
There is also an important distinction here. The target is the software being evaluated.

32
00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:39.280
A claim source is a seller document describing that software.

33
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:44.080
The repository is evidence used to test claims. It is not itself a claim source.

34
00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:48.820
Why freeze the claims before looking at the evidence?

35
00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:52.800
Because otherwise the evidence can influence what gets tested.

36
00:02:53.520 --> 00:02:59.100
We first identify the seller's claims. The buyer can also add specific claims they want tested.

37
00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:04.380
That list is reviewed and settled before we start deciding whether any claim holds.

38
00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:07.540
Only then does the evidence review begin.

39
00:03:08.040 --> 00:03:14.020
This prevents a subtle but important problem, choosing the questions after you have started seeing the answers.

40
00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.300
A review should not become more interested in the easy claims

41
00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:22.480
because they are easy to support or quietly lose interest in awkward ones.

42
00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:26.400
Every frozen claim gets attempted and gets its own finding.

43
00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:30.390
So what counts as evidence for a finding?

44
00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.150
It depends on what the claim says.

45
00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:36.890
A useful citation has to do more than point to a file.

46
00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:42.290
It has to bear on the particular claim and the finding records what that citation shows.

47
00:03:43.090 --> 00:03:44.830
Some claims require broader evidence.

48
00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.910
Suppose the seller says, we store only the hit count and nothing else.

49
00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:54.530
Finding the database code that increments the hit count supports one part of that statement.

50
00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.250
It does not prove the words and nothing else.

51
00:03:58.330 --> 00:04:01.510
To support that part, the review has to look elsewhere too.

52
00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:07.390
For logging, telemetry, network calls, or other places where visitor information might be recorded.

53
00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:10.530
The claim determines the evidence required.

54
00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:15.270
The demo contains exactly that kind of example.

55
00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:19.030
The seller says that only the hit is recorded and nothing else.

56
00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.430
The database code supports part of the statement.

57
00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:26.450
It increments the hit counter without adding other information to that table.

58
00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:32.190
But elsewhere, the server's default access log records the client's address and user agent.

59
00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:36.510
So the useful conclusion is not simply true or false.

60
00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:42.490
The evidence shows that the claim is partly true and the report preserves both sides of that finding.

61
00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:48.050
Whether the difference matters commercially depends on what the buyer is relying on.

62
00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:52.730
For a buyer paying for a particular privacy posture, it may matter a great deal.

63
00:04:54.010 --> 00:04:57.290
The first review is performed with AI assistance.

64
00:04:57.910 --> 00:05:00.310
Who checks whether its findings are actually sound?

65
00:05:01.750 --> 00:05:05.290
A separate check examines each finished finding against its evidence.

66
00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:07.910
It asks several distinct questions.

67
00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.170
Did we state the seller's claim accurately?

68
00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:13.610
Does the cited material actually relate to that claim?

69
00:05:14.810 --> 00:05:16.830
Does it show what the finding says it shows?

70
00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:19.510
Is the verdict appropriate for the evidence?

71
00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:24.370
And when searches were required, were they broad enough to find what mattered?

72
00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:30.250
This is not a second attempt to redo the whole review or find additional faults in the target.

73
00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:32.750
It is a check on the issued findings themselves.

74
00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:37.890
Some findings are also retested, including findings the first check accepted.

75
00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:42.790
That helps detect both false alarms and problems the checker may have missed.

76
00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.730
What happens when the evidence simply can't answer the question?

77
00:05:48.550 --> 00:05:51.310
That is kept separate from finding a problem.

78
00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:58.270
If the evidence shows that a seller claim is wrong, partly true, or needs an important qualification,

79
00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:03.950
the report rates the materiality of the gap, what that demonstrated difference would change for this buyer.

80
00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:09.610
If the supplied materials cannot settle the claim, nothing has been found against the seller.

81
00:06:10.610 --> 00:06:17.590
Instead, the report describes the exposure, how much the buyer is relying on something that still has not been verified.

82
00:06:18.010 --> 00:06:19.170
That distinction matters.

83
00:06:19.990 --> 00:06:24.890
We found a problem, and we could not verify this, are not the same conclusion,

84
00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:27.550
and the report should never make them sound like one.

85
00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.480
Once I have the report, is that the end of the engagement?

86
00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:34.960
The review itself is finished.

87
00:06:35.480 --> 00:06:38.700
The findings are issued and are not quietly rewritten afterward.

88
00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:42.400
But the delivered product remains available for questions.

89
00:06:43.140 --> 00:06:47.400
You can ask why a finding received its verdict, what evidence supports it,

90
00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:52.080
why something was rated material, what might settle an unresolved claim,

91
00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.280
or what parts of the target the review did not examine.

92
00:06:56.340 --> 00:07:02.440
The continuation works from the completed report, the working record behind it, and the cited target material.

93
00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:09.040
And when the completed review does not answer a question, it says so rather than inventing an answer.

94
00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:16.860
So the report is not merely something you receive and file away, it remains available as a way to explore the completed review.

95
00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:22.120
What kind of diligence is this meant to replace?

96
00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:25.080
Usually, none of the large categories of diligence.

97
00:07:25.700 --> 00:07:27.340
Tuuyi is not a penetration test.

98
00:07:27.340 --> 00:07:33.880
It is not a general code quality score, an architecture assessment, legal advice, or a valuation opinion.

99
00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:35.580
It has a narrower purpose.

100
00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:40.820
Examine claims about software that a buyer or advisor is being asked to rely on,

101
00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:45.040
test those claims against available evidence, and make the results inspectable.

102
00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:52.120
That can fit small software acquisitions, claims about AI technology, acceptance of contracted software,

103
00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:57.880
or evaluating an open-source dependency when important decisions rest on what its documentation says.

104
00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:00.760
Sometimes a claims review may be enough.

105
00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:04.920
Sometimes it identifies exactly where deeper diligence is warranted.

106
00:08:07.240 --> 00:08:09.440
A Tuuyi engagement is deliberately bounded.

107
00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:15.840
The client supplies a code snapshot or repository, identifies the seller documents whose claims matter,

108
00:08:16.200 --> 00:08:20.780
and provides the deal context needed to understand which differences would matter commercially.

109
00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:26.980
Tuuyi returns the claims and verdicts, the evidence behind each finding, the independent check,

110
00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:32.340
buyer-specific materiality or exposure ratings, and questions that remain for the seller.

111
00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:39.000
After delivery, the private continuation page remains available for questions about the completed review.

112
00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:43.400
The aim is not to pronounce on everything about a company or its software.

113
00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:44.820
It is more specific.

114
00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:51.060
Reduce the amount of a software decision that rests on claims nobody has actually checked.
